    "content": "You are comparing two texts written in response to the same task. Decide which one reads MORE like unedited, default output from an AI language model.\n\nJudge ONLY the presence of the tells listed below. Do not judge argument quality, factual accuracy, length, formatting preference, or whether you agree with the content. A longer, better-argued or more polished text is not more human. Pick the text with MORE tells.\n\nTELLS OF UNEDITED AI PROSE (judge on these only)\n\nStructure\n1. Negative parallelism used as a device: \"It's not X. It's Y.\" / \"not just X, but Y\" / \"isn't about X, it's about Y\", especially more than once.\n2. Signposting: the text announces what it is about to do (\"Let's break this down\", \"Here's the thing\", \"Here's why\", \"The bottom line\", \"The takeaway\").\n3. Self-applause: \"Let that sink in\", \"This changes everything\", \"Read that again\", \"Make no mistake\".\n4. Recap endings: a closing paragraph that restates the body (\"In conclusion\", \"Ultimately\", \"At the end of the day\", \"In short\").\n5. Bold-label paragraph openers (\"**The problem.** ...\") and headers, bullets or numbered lists where prose was asked for.\n6. Triads everywhere: lists of exactly three items or three adjectives, sentence after sentence.\n7. Participial tails: sentences ending in \", making it ...\", \", ensuring that ...\", \", allowing teams to ...\".\n8. Invented labels for ordinary observations (\"I call this the Clarity Flywheel\").\n9. Uniform rhythm: every sentence about the same length, or relentless short punchy fragments. Real writing has one sentence that runs on and one short one.\n10. Rhetorical question followed by its own answer (\"The result? Nobody used it.\").\n\nWords\n11. Overused vocabulary: delve, tapestry, myriad, underscore, seamless, robust, foster, elevate, unlock, testament, landscape, navigate, crucial, pivotal, leverage, harness, realm, embark, journey, transformative, paradigm, ever-evolving, multifaceted, holistic, resonate, empower, unleash, beacon, intricate, nuanced, vibrant, meticulous, streamline, game-changer, cutting-edge, actionable, supercharge, north star, move the needle, \"in today's fast-paced world\", \"it's worth noting\", \"plays a crucial role\".\n12. Filler transitions at sentence start: However, Additionally, Furthermore, Moreover, That said, Ultimately, Importantly, Indeed, Of course.\n13. Em dashes used as the default joint between clauses.\n14. Artificial enthusiasm: \"thrilled to announce\", \"excited to share\", \"we've got you covered\", \"say goodbye to\", \"buckle up\".\n\nReader\n15. Performing for an imagined crowd rather than talking to one person; nothing specific (no number, name, date, or concrete detail) for several sentences in a row.\n16. Generic or manufactured anecdotes: a story with no specific details that could only be true of one person.\n17. Every point lands, every ending uplifts; the author is never irritated, uncertain, or wrong about anything.\n18. Explaining what the reader could infer from the previous sentence (\"what this means is ...\").\n\n\nTASK GIVEN TO BOTH WRITERS:\nWrite an essay of up to 500 words on why most product roadmaps fail.\n\n=== TEXT A ===\nMost product roadmaps fail because they pretend we know more about the future than we do. Teams gather in a room, look at some data, listen to a few loud stakeholders, and then draw a line of features stretching six or twelve months ahead as if customer behavior, competitors, and technical surprises will all cooperate. They never do. The document that was supposed to create alignment quickly becomes a source of disappointment and quiet resentment.\n\nThe bigger problem is how we treat the thing once it exists. A roadmap gets treated like a promise instead of a current best guess. Dates and features turn into commitments that people are afraid to change even when the original assumptions have already proven wrong. Updating it feels like admitting failure, so it sits there getting more and more disconnected from reality while everyone pretends they are still following it.\n\nWe also fill these documents with the wrong content. Feature after feature gets listed because features are easy to argue about and easy to show in a slide. Outcomes, customer problems, and the actual bets we are making get less airtime. When users surprise us or the market moves, the feature list still looks official even though it no longer points at anything useful.\n\nA lot of roadmaps are created without the people who have to live with them. Product managers and executives produce a neat timeline, then hand it to engineering and design as if execution is just a detail. The people closest to the work see the holes immediately, the hidden dependencies, the technical debt that was never mentioned, the research that still needs to happen. They never really own the plan, so they work around it.\n\nTimelines are almost always optimistic to the point of fiction. We forget how long discovery actually takes, how many meetings will interrupt the work, and how often the first approach will not work. Then we feel pressure to look aggressive so we cut the buffers. The result is a calendar that starts slipping in week two.\n\nPriorities at the top of the company change faster than any roadmap can keep up. A new executive arrives, a competitor launches something, a big customer makes a demand. The old items never get formally killed. They just linger, creating a document that is half strategy and half political leftover.\n\nThe ones that work better are usually messier and more honest. They talk about themes and questions instead of a sequence of deliverables. They get revisited often enough that people still trust them. Most teams never quite get there because looking certain feels safer than looking like you are still figuring it out.\n=== END OF TEXT A ===\n\n=== TEXT B ===\n**Why Most Product Roadmaps Fail**\n\nProduct roadmaps are meant to align teams, communicate strategy, and sequence work toward meaningful outcomes. In practice most fail, producing missed dates, unused features, eroded trust, and wasted effort. The failures are rarely about the tool itself; they stem from how organizations treat planning under uncertainty.\n\nA common root cause is the missing link to strategy. Roadmaps often become feature wish lists assembled from sales requests, executive opinions, and competitor mimicry rather than from a clear product vision and measurable business goals. Without that filter, teams ship work that does not move customer value or revenue. Prioritization then defaults to politics or recency instead of evidence.\n\nEqually damaging is treating the roadmap as a contract rather than a communication artifact. Software development involves discovery, technical unknowns, and shifting markets. Publishing detailed dates and specifications quarters or years ahead creates false certainty. When reality intervenes, the plan is either ignored or defended at the cost of better opportunities. Stakeholders who were sold a schedule feel betrayed.\n\nMany roadmaps also confuse outputs with outcomes. A list of “add reporting dashboard” or “redesign onboarding” describes what will be built, not the user or business problem to be solved. Without continuous customer research and validation, teams optimize for delivery velocity instead of learning. Features ship, usage stays low, and the next cycle repeats the same pattern.\n\nStakeholder misalignment compounds these issues. Different functions optimize for different metrics: sales for closing deals, engineering for technical elegance, executives for visible progress. Absent a shared framework and regular, transparent trade-off conversations, everything is labeled high priority. Capacity is overcommitted, quality suffers, and morale declines.\n\nFinally, roadmaps stagnate. Once published they are rarely revisited with new data. Markets, competitors, and user behavior change; a static document does not. Cross-functional reviews that treat the roadmap as a living hypothesis—rather than a sacred plan—are the exception, not the rule.\n\nEffective roadmaps are therefore outcome-oriented, time-horizon-aware (now/next/later rather than dated Gantt charts), evidence-based, and frequently updated. They communicate intent and trade-offs, not promises. Organizations that treat them this way still encounter surprises, but they adapt instead of failing by design. Those that do not continue to produce elaborate documents that no one follows.\n=== END OF TEXT B ===\n\nAnswer with a single JSON object and nothing else:\n{\"more_ai\": \"A\" or \"B\", \"confidence\": 1-5, \"tells_A\": [\"short tell\", ...], \"tells_B\": [\"short tell\", ...]}\n"
